  "preamble": "Intervocalic [j] is very prone to loss. Proto-Greek and Proto-Italic lost inherited intervocalic *y before the historical period, causing vowel contraction (*tréyes > τρεῖς, *monéyeti > moneō). Modern New Mexican and northern Mexican Spanish delete intervocalic /ʝ/ (realized as a glide) next to front vowels, and in French [j] from intervocalic g was absorbed into a preceding vowel. In the Southwestern Paman languages Uw-Oykangand and Uw-Olgol, initial *y was lost along with other initial consonants (alh 'fire' < *yalyu 'flame'). Word-initial *y was lost in Tamil, Malayalam, Kannada and Tulu (Proto-Dravidian *yĀnay 'elephant' > Tamil āṉai, Kannada āne), with older y-forms surviving as Tamil yāṉai and Kannada yāne.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Elision, Glide Loss, Vowel Contraction",
